        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br><div>
The microgravitational condition affects human brain physiology and cognitive functioning. Astronauts returning from spaceflight have shown a number of cognitive impairment including tension, depression, anxiety, and withdrawal during and after the space flight. This phenomenon has majorly affected their health and decision-making process during and after the mission. Therefore, a great deal of mental health monitoring system has been implemented pre and post-mission for astronauts. However, presently there is no physiological marker (biomarker) that would detect their mental health condition early on. In this regard, it is worth mentioning that the Reward System (RS) deregulation in the brain can be one of the early indicators of neuronal health . RS of the brain is one of the major neuronal networks that involves many neurocognitive functions. The primary neurotransmitter that is involved in RS is dopamine (DA). DA is associated with various brain functions including emotion, cognition, pleasure, memory etc. It is also known that DJ1 (PARK7 gene) protein regulates DA through DA transporter. DJ1 is a highly conserved neuroprotective protein that was initially linked with autosomal recessive Parkinson's disease. DJ1's role in controlling reward response in the brain has recently been investigated by us and few others. For normal brain function, DJ1 must maintain the proper balance of DA among neurotransmitters. However, DJ1 functioning in the microgravity condition has not been investigated. Therefore, We hypothesize that DJ1 will serve as a potential biomarker for early detection of impaired reward system in microgravity. This project will generate a proof-of-concept to identify the role of DJ1 in DA release that will be indicative of impaired RS in the brain in microgravity condition. <br></div><div><br></div><p>
APPROACH:<br>
In order to test our hypothesis in a mouse model, we will be requesting tissue sample from deceased mouse those were exposed to spaceflight condition in the past spaceflight mission. For negative control for the study, we will use tissue from the mice those were lived in earth condition. A comparative analysis of DJ1 function in microgravity conditions and earth condition will give a clear indication of its role in RS functioning in space environment, which affects directly affects the neurocognitive and associated behaviors. Therefore, the present project will be done in two steps.</p><p>Task-1: To study the transcriptional and translational expressions of DJ1 in mouse brain tissues. BALB/cJ mice brain tissues and blood pellets will be requested from LSDA to perform the gene, protein and sequence analysis for DJ1 expression. 
<br>
Task-2: To determine the effect of DJ1 on DA regulation in the space flight condition. Brain tissues from BALB/cJ mice will be used for immunohistochemistry to study the DA and DJ1 expression. This will help us to establish the relation between DJ1 expression and DA release. 
</p><p>
Methods: Quantitative real-time PCR (qRT-PCR): Gene expression will be quantitated using real-time PCR as per previous publication from control and space flight exposed mouse brain tissues and blood cells. RNA from cell pellets will be extracted using the RNAeasy mini kit (Qiagen, Germany). Following that the cDNA synthesis will be performed using cDNA kit (Applied Biosystems) to perform qRT-PCR using TaqMan gene expression assays (Applied Biosystems) for DJ1 and GAPDH. Relative mRNA species expression will be quantitated and the mean fold change in expression of DJ1 gene will be calculated using the comparative CT method (Transcript Accumulation Index, TAI = 2-??CT). 
</p><p>
DJ1 Protein Levels by Western Blot  (WB) Analysis: Same amounts of brain tissues will be extracted for protein analysis from control and spaceflight exposed mouse. All the protein samples will be  separated by using Mini-Protean TGX Precast Gels (Bio-Rad) as per previous publication. ImageJ software will be used to quantify the protein expression on the blot and will be expressed as a fold change with respect to treatment. 
</p><p>
Immunohistochemistry and tract tracing of DA: The following antibodies will be used for immunohistochemistry studies: Tyrosine hydroxylase (TH), the rate-limiting enzyme for catecholamine synthesis, will serve as a marker for DA neurons; Fluorogold (FG), a neuronal retrograde tract tracer; and DJ1 protein. Our experiments are designed to determine the expression pattern of DJ1 in the ventral tegmental area (VTA) and the degree of co-localization of TH and DJ1. The retrograde tracer FG will be infused into the nucleus accumbens (NAcc). FG is typically absorbed by presynaptic terminals and transported to the cell body. Therefore, using immunohistochemistry, we will be able to visualize DANs that send axons to the NAcc. If a cell in the VTA stains positive for TH, FG, and DJ1, then that would indicate that this particular DJ1-expressing DAN projects to the NAcc, which will be a major breakthrough in our field. 
</p><p>
RESULTS:<br>
This experiment is currently in progress.  Results will be available at the conclusion of the study.</p>
